        "category": "Hands-on Science • Making a Balance",
        "q": "How can you construct a simple working beam balance using a plastic ruler, thread, and paper cups?",
        "ans": "<strong>Construction of a Simple Beam Balance:</strong><br>\n1. <strong>Pivot (Fulcrum):</strong> Punch a tiny hole at the exact centre (15 cm mark) of a 30 cm plastic ruler and suspend it with a looped piece of thread from a stationary hook so it hangs horizontally balanced.<br>\n2. <strong>Pans:</strong> Punch three equidistant holes near the rims of two identical paper cups. Tie three equal-length threads to each cup and hang one cup at the 2 cm mark and the other at the 28 cm mark.<br>\n3. <strong>Operation:</strong> When both pans are empty, the ruler stays level. Place an unknown object (like an eraser) in one pan and known standard units (like metal paper clips or identical coins) in the other pan until the ruler balances horizontally.",
